		id: 'what-propitiation-means',
		heading: 'What propitiation means',
		bodyHtml: `<p><em>Propitiation</em> is an old English word for <strong>a sacrifice that satisfies justice and turns away wrath, so a broken relationship can be restored</strong>. It answers a real problem: sin is serious, and a holy Father does not pretend it is not.</p><p>But the Bible turns the pagan idea of appeasing an angry god upside down. People do not bribe our Father into kindness; our Father, out of love, provides the sacrifice. &ldquo;This is love: not that we loved our Father, but that he loved us and sent his Son as the atoning sacrifice for our sins&rdquo; (1 John 4:10).</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'greek-and-hebrew',
		heading: 'The Greek and Hebrew words',
		bodyHtml: `<p>The Greek <em>hilasmos</em> (1 John 2:2; 4:10) means an atoning sacrifice. <em>Hilast&#275;rion</em> (Romans 3:25) is the word the Greek Old Testament used for the <strong>mercy seat</strong>, the gold lid of the ark of the covenant. The related verb appears in Hebrews 2:17, which the FHB renders &ldquo;clearing away the people&rsquo;s sins.&rdquo;</p><p>Behind them stands the Hebrew <em>kipper</em>, to atone or cover, and <em>kapporet</em>, the mercy seat itself. The Father&rsquo;s Heart Bible&trade; uses the plain phrase &ldquo;atoning sacrifice&rdquo; so the meaning is clear without a dictionary.</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'the-mercy-seat',
		heading: 'The mercy seat',
		bodyHtml: `<p>On the Day of Atonement the high priest carried blood &ldquo;inside the curtain&rdquo; and sprinkled it &ldquo;on the mercy seat and before it&rdquo; (Leviticus 16:15). That lid was where our Father had promised to meet His people: &ldquo;I will meet you there and speak with you above the mercy seat, between the two cherubim on the ark of the testimony&rdquo; (Exodus 25:22).</p><p>So when Paul calls Jesus the <em>hilast&#275;rion</em>, he is saying Jesus is the place where sin is covered and our Father meets us: &ldquo;Our Father presented him as the atoning sacrifice, through faith in his blood&rdquo; (Romans 3:25).</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'for-the-whole-world',
		heading: 'For the whole world',
		bodyHtml: `<p>John says the reach of it is without limit: &ldquo;He himself is the atoning sacrifice for our sins, and not ours only but also the whole world&rsquo;s&rdquo; (1 John 2:2). Just before, he calls Jesus our advocate: &ldquo;if anyone does sin, we have an advocate before the Father&mdash;Jesus Christ, the righteous one&rdquo; (1 John 2:1).</p><p>Paul adds that it shows our Father is both just and generous: He does this &ldquo;so he is both just and the justifier of whoever trusts in Jesus&rdquo; (Romans 3:26).</p>`,
	},
	{
		id: 'why-it-is-good-news',
		heading: 'Why it is good news',
		bodyHtml: `<p>Propitiation means guilt does not have the last word. Sin is not ignored; it is dealt with, fully, by the Son our Father sent. That is why Romans 3 can say we &ldquo;are justified freely by his grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus&rdquo; (Romans 3:24).</p><p>The FHB footnote on 1 John 2:2 puts it plainly: Jesus is the sacrifice that covers our sin and turns away judgment, offered for the whole world. Children who were far away can come home.</p>`,
	},
];

